Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether there exists a sequence of polynomials $\{p_n\}$ such that $p_n(x) \to \cos(1/x)$ for every $x \in (0, \infty)$.

**Answer: Yes.**

## Proof

The key observation is that $\cos(1/x)$ is continuous on all of $(0, \infty)$ (the only problematic point, $x = 0$, lies outside the domain). We exploit this together with the Weierstrass approximation theorem on an exhausting family of compact subintervals.

**Construction.** For each integer $n \geq 1$, define the compact interval
$$K_n = \left[\tfrac{1}{n},\, n\right] \subset (0, \infty).$$

Since $1/x$ is continuous on $K_n$ (as $x \geq 1/n > 0$) and $\cos$ is continuous, the composition $f(x) = \cos(1/x)$ is continuous on $K_n$. By the **Weierstrass approximation theorem**, there exists a polynomial $p_n$ such that
$$\sup_{x \in K_n} |p_n(x) - \cos(1/x)| < \frac{1}{n}. \tag{1}$$

**Pointwise convergence.** Fix any $x_0 \in (0, \infty)$. Choose an integer $N$ with $N > \max(x_0,\, 1/x_0)$. Then for every $n \geq N$:
- $\frac{1}{n} \leq \frac{1}{N} < x_0$, so $x_0 \geq \frac{1}{n}$;
- $n \geq N > x_0$, so $x_0 \leq n$.

Hence $x_0 \in K_n$ for all $n \geq N$. Applying $(1)$:
$$|p_n(x_0) - \cos(1/x_0)| < \frac{1}{n} \quad \text{for all } n \geq N.$$

As $n \to \infty$, the right-hand side tends to $0$, so
$$p_n(x_0) \longrightarrow \cos(1/x_0).$$

Since $x_0 \in (0, \infty)$ was arbitrary, we conclude
$$p_n(x) \to \cos(1/x) \quad \text{pointwise on } (0, \infty). \qquad \blacksquare$$

**Remark.** This argument uses nothing specific to $\cos(1/x)$ beyond its continuity on $(0,\infty)$. In fact, the same proof shows that *every* continuous function on $(0,\infty)$ — regardless of how wildly it oscillates near $0$ or how it grows near $\infty$ — can be approximated pointwise by a sequence of polynomials. The essential point is that pointwise convergence is weak enough that uniform approximation on each compact piece (via Weierstrass) suffices; the "bad" behavior near $0$ and $\infty$ is pushed off to later and later terms in the sequence, never affecting any fixed point.

### PROOF COMPLETE
